Mount a file-backed Btrfs loop device:
Create a sparse file (uses no space initially): 
mkdir -p /usr/local/google/btrfs_img
truncate -s 1T /usr/local/google/btrfs_img/btrfs.img


Format it: 
sudo mkfs.btrfs -f /usr/local/google/btrfs_img/btrfs.img


Add the following to /etc/fstab (not run as a terminal command):
/usr/local/google/btrfs_img/btrfs.img /usr/local/google/btrfs_mount btrfs loop,nofail,compress=zstd:3,discard=async 0 0


Mount it (and set permissions): 
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
mkdir -p /usr/local/google/btrfs_mount
sudo mount /usr/local/google/btrfs_mount
sudo chown -R $USER: /usr/local/google/btrfs_mount




Create the subvolume
cd /usr/local/google/btrfs_mount 
btrfs subvolume create chromium_base 


Set up a Chromium repo clone as normal
cd chromium_base
fetch chromium # or cp an existing clone
gn gen out/Default # or cp existing gn args, etc.




Create a snapshot (in /usr/local/google/btrfs_mount):
btrfs subvolume snapshot ./chromium_base ./my_cool_feature

or butter yet, use Chromium’s depot_tools:
gclient-new-workdir.py ./chromium_base ./my_cool_feature


Delete a snapshot:
btrfs subvolume delete ./my_cool_feature
